    title_en: "Hackers Release New Defender Zero-day ShieldBreak: Windows Privilege Escalation Bypassing Patches",
    summary_en: "Security researcher Nightmare Eclipse released a Defender zero-day vulnerability called ShieldBreak. This vulnerability is reported to bypass Microsoft's patch for a previous flaw, CVE-2026-50656, allowing attackers to gain SYSTEM privileges on fully patched Windows 10, Windows 11, and Windows Server systems. Security expert Kevin Beaumont confirmed the vulnerability's feasibility and provided relevant detection queries, advising defenders to use these queries before Microsoft releases a fix. ShieldBreak is a local privilege-escalation vulnerability that operates by modifying file contents using a user-mode callback hook during the Cloud Filter API (cfapi) process in Defender cloud data processing scans. Although Nightmare claims this targets a bypass for CVE-2026-50656, Beaumont noted a significant difference in the mechanisms of the two. Microsoft is aware of this vulnerability and stated that it is actively investigating its validity and applicability, while also emphasizing support for coordinated vulnerability disclosure standards. The emergence of this vulnerability highlights the trend of attackers continuously seeking zero-day flaws that bypass patches.",
