    summary_en: "In the Windows 11 security update released by Microsoft in August 2026 (KB5121003, Build 26200.9168+), the 'Low Latency Profile' feature extends CPU enhancement technology from system shell elements (such as the Start Menu and Search) to the application startup process. The principle behind this technology is the 'race to sleep' mechanism: when a user interacts with the system, the CPU instantly spikes to near maximum frequency. After quickly completing high-load tasks, it can enter a deep idle state faster than traditional methods, achieving both lower power consumption and faster responsiveness. The article notes that while this feature was introduced in June 2026, it only officially enabled application support with this update. The affected product is the Windows 11 operating system. Users who have not yet received this feature via 'Controlled Feature Rollout' can manually enable it using the third-party open-source tool ViVeTool with specific commands. Users are advised to wait for Microsoft to gradually push the feature through official channels to ensure system stability.",
